A `for … else` in the `@change` handler of a **package-level** var.

`blocks.go` enumerates the bodies a pass may rewrite, and it used to name the
package's funcs and its body and stop -- `pkg.Vars[].Handlers` was in nobody's
list, while a component's and a window's were. So this else block reached
codegen undesugared and every backend dropped it in silence, which is the exact
failure `for_else_imperative.txtar` exists to deny one level down.

It is not a shape nothing produces: nothing moves a package's vars off the
package, so a top-level `var` with a handler survives to the end of the
pipeline with its handler attached.

The enumeration now asks `ir.Owners`, which says a package owns its vars the
way a component and a window own theirs, so the pass reaches it because the
answer is written down once rather than because this file remembered.

deny * `__ran0 = true;?\n\s*\}` -- the flag is the body's first statement, never its last

-- app.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"

output {
    go {
        bubbletea
    }
    none {
        html
    }
}

var xs list<int> = []
var note = ""

var count = 0 @change {
    for var x = xs {
        note = note + string(x)
    } else {
        note = "empty"
    }
}

window {
    button(text="bump", @click {
        count = count + 1
    })
    text(value=note)
}
